      <h1>Ring or wrist: another way to go screenless</h1>
      <p class="meta">
        <time datetime="2026-10-26">26 October 2026</time>
        <a href="{% if blogs.news.handle != blank %}{{ blogs.news.url }}{% elsif pages.journal.url != blank %}{{ pages.journal.url }}{% else %}{{ routes.all_products_collection_url }}?view=journal{% endif %}">All notes</a>
      </p>
      <p class="lede">A short pointer for “Oura alternative” searches. Form-factor tradeoffs and the HOOP fact table are on the compare hub.</p>
    </header>

    <p>Oura is a ring. NEXTBODY Hoop is a wrist band with no screen. A ring leaves the wrist free. A band can run HOOP’s Body Scan: hold the side key for about 30 seconds for 14 bioimpedance estimates. That is a different tradeoff, not a claim that a ring is worse.</p>
    <p>The product site includes one same-person, same-night wear with an Oura Ring. Total sleep agreed to 99.5% (awake time excluded). The notes call this a single result, not a clinical finding. HOOP’s seven-factor sleep score draws on Oura’s published sleep factors but is not Oura’s algorithm.</p>
    <p>Do not use Body Scan with a pacemaker or other implanted electronic medical device. HOOP is not a medical device. The published hardware price is $59.99 on the pricing page.</p>
    <p>If the search was really “WHOOP alternative no subscription”, or you want the strap-off weight chart next to sleep and MCP limits, use the hub. This note stays short on purpose.</p>

    <p class="note">Oura, Oura Ring and other marks belong to their owners. Mention is descriptive only. <a href="{% if pages['screenless-fitness-band'].url != blank %}{{ pages['screenless-fitness-band'].url }}{% else %}{{ routes.all_products_collection_url }}?view=compare{% endif %}">Open the screenless-band compare hub.</a></p>
